WEBVTT

00:00:00.413 --> 00:00:01.353
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Part 1

00:00:07.408 --> 00:00:10.627
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> Today, I’m talking to acclaimed playwright Diane Samuels,

00:00:10.907 --> 00:00:14.772
whose play <i>Kindertransport</i> is widely considered a modern classic

00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:18.394
and is now a set exam text for secondary school students.

00:00:18.893 --> 00:00:19.468
<v><b>Diane:</b></v> Hello.

00:00:20.135 --> 00:00:23.485
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> Diane, you were formerly a drama teacher, is that right?

00:00:23.652 --> 00:00:25.094
<v><b>Diane:</b></v> Yes, that’s right.

00:00:25.553 --> 00:00:30.308
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> Earlier this week, you may have heard one of our most respected Shakespearean actresses,

00:00:30.395 --> 00:00:34.801
Dame Helen Mirren, announce somewhat controversially in a TV interview

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:38.569
that it’s a waste of time for children to read Shakespeare plays at school,

00:00:38.855 --> 00:00:42.945
that Shakespeare must be brought alive through watching plays or films.

00:00:43.379 --> 00:00:48.519
At the same time, some recently published research carried out in four large

00:00:48.586 --> 00:00:51.430
London secondary schools tells a different story.

00:00:51.816 --> 00:00:54.615
It concludes that, even though, inevitably,

00:00:54.835 --> 00:00:58.309
some students think Shakespeare is boring and irrelevant,

00:00:58.556 --> 00:01:03.019
many others say that they really enjoy reading and studying Shakespeare

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:07.130
– and these students don’t see the complexity of his language as a barrier,

00:01:07.396 --> 00:01:09.482
more as an interesting challenge.

00:01:09.908 --> 00:01:14.201
Diane, can I ask you, first of all, did you study Shakespeare at school?

00:01:14.508 --> 00:01:16.900
Did you have a good or bad experience?

00:01:17.553 --> 00:01:21.751
<v><b>Diane:</b></v> I remember studying <i>Macbeth</i> when I was about 15,

00:01:22.218 --> 00:01:27.130
and I studied <i>Hamlet</i> for A level, and <i>King Lear</i>.

00:01:27.463 --> 00:01:32.724
It was in the 1970s, so – in a Jewish comprehensive school in Liverpool,

00:01:32.951 --> 00:01:38.065
and we would read the play aloud and talk about it a bit.

00:01:38.505 --> 00:01:45.531
We didn’t act any of it out really, but I just…I did enjoy reading it aloud together in class.

00:01:46.151 --> 00:01:48.008
It’s a very simple thing to do,

00:01:48.061 --> 00:01:51.293
but you know that feeling of just reading something and airing it together?

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.586
I mean, there’s always the issue, isn’t there, of who gets which roles to read,

00:01:54.626 --> 00:01:56.969
some people were more confident readers than others.

00:01:59.175 --> 00:02:05.076
So, it was enjoyable, and I also...one of the other things that I remember was, for exams,

00:02:05.076 --> 00:02:09.428
you obviously needed to remember the play, and I did enjoy

00:02:10.782 --> 00:02:12.599
– maybe I enjoyed this more than anything

00:02:12.626 --> 00:02:17.787
– learning soliloquies or learning quotes and kind of walking around my bedroom

00:02:17.787 --> 00:02:21.573
or walking to school saying them over to myself.

00:02:22.386 --> 00:02:25.236
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> And did you teach Shakespeare when you were a drama teacher?

00:02:25.648 --> 00:02:32.412
<v><b>Diane:</b></v> When I was a drama teacher, I wasn’t required to teach Shakespeare at all.

00:02:32.505 --> 00:02:37.775
I taught in inner London secondary schools in the 1980s

00:02:38.142 --> 00:02:47.315
and Shakespeare wasn’t on the GCSE syllabus, as it was just coming in in those days.

00:02:47.462 --> 00:02:50.962
So, you weren’t required to teach Shakespeare at all in drama.

00:02:51.009 --> 00:02:51.569
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> Incredible.

00:02:54.031 --> 00:02:57.576
<v><b>Diane:</b></v> But I did … I wanted to bring it to the students.

00:02:57.723 --> 00:03:00.922
I taught for three years in a school called Hackney Downs,

00:03:01.075 --> 00:03:04.347
which was a boys’ school in Hackney – it doesn’t exist anymore

00:03:04.955 --> 00:03:09.891
– and...I’m always exploring the dynamics of it.

00:03:09.977 --> 00:03:18.770
I like choosing a very particular scenario and then letting the whole play come alive through that scenario.

00:03:18.816 --> 00:03:23.146
So, what I did was, I went into role as Desdemona,

00:03:23.366 --> 00:03:26.441
and I didn’t tell them anything about Othello, about the play,

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:32.390
and I just sat in front of these 15-year-old boys and I put them in role and I said,

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.442
‘I want you to come to...to give advice.

00:03:37.488 --> 00:03:42.082
I want you to be like counsellors or advisers for this young woman who’s got a problem.’

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:44.253
And I didn’t tell them any more than that,

00:03:44.546 --> 00:03:48.278
and then I told them about how my handkerchief had gone missing,

00:03:48.372 --> 00:03:52.814
and what would I do if my husband had given it to me,

00:03:52.873 --> 00:03:58.507
and we just got into the play by this very little thing.

00:03:58.628 --> 00:04:00.254
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> How did your students respond?

00:04:00.813 --> 00:04:02.590
<v><b>Diane:</b></v> They just came to all these conclusions,

00:04:02.637 --> 00:04:06.136
and they ended up actually voicing the things Othello voiced, like,

00:04:06.869 --> 00:04:09.524
‘Well, are you having an affair with Cassio?’

00:04:10.258 --> 00:04:12.056
And they became really suspicious,

00:04:12.143 --> 00:04:16.166
and I didn’t do anything but ask their advice about the handkerchief,

00:04:16.215 --> 00:04:20.874
and it was very interesting, the way the dynamic of the play,

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:27.561
just through this one character with this problem, sort of came alive in the role-play,

00:04:27.654 --> 00:04:29.140
and I really enjoyed doing that.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:35.121
And they then wanted to know a lot more about the play, but they were not coming at it as a text.

00:04:35.494 --> 00:04:43.423
They were coming at it as a kind of living thing that was about people with struggles,

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:49.687
and it was very enjoyable and they really completely got it, it was brilliant, on an emotional level.

00:04:50.114 --> 00:04:54.510
So they went at it, if you like, emotionally and physically rather than intellectually.

00:04:54.584 --> 00:04:55.511
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> Absolutely.

00:04:55.910 --> 00:05:00.933
So what do you think about Helen Mirren’s opinion, that reading Shakespeare in the classroom is a waste of time?

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:08.038
<v><b>Diane:</b></v> I’m always cautious, in terms of learning,

00:05:08.404 --> 00:05:14.403
to say anything is a waste of time, ’cause it depends how you do it.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:22.670
I’m sure Helen Mirren, coming at it as an actress, is in a way not saying it’s a waste of time,

00:05:22.676 --> 00:05:25.052
but there’s better things you could be doing with your time.

00:05:25.159 --> 00:05:30.827
And I have to say, in teaching Shakespeare, I do think what you want to do is get to the drama.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:39.020
Yeah, it is live theatre and I would always err on the side of going for it on the floor,

00:05:39.066 --> 00:05:44.811
if you like, first, and I tend to start a session, personally, working with Shakespeare,

00:05:46.079 --> 00:05:48.054
getting people standing up in a space.

00:05:48.081 --> 00:05:53.549
But then, I was trained as a drama teacher, and I make theatre, and I,

00:05:53.674 --> 00:06:00.849
in my writing workshops that I run for all ages, I tend to get people playing and moving around,

00:06:00.929 --> 00:06:04.830
or if they’re sitting around I get them into active-mode writing.

00:06:05.383 --> 00:06:13.892
So, yeah, I think bringing it alive, getting into…the characters are great,

00:06:13.991 --> 00:06:19.431
you know…getting into the language as it lives is probably more exciting

00:06:19.464 --> 00:06:22.979
and multidimensional than reading it off the page.

00:06:23.031 --> 00:06:26.153
But then there’s moments when just sitting quietly

00:06:26.686 --> 00:06:30.347
and sitting with the language and looking at it can be fruitful, too,

00:06:30.567 --> 00:06:36.687
so I think there’s probably a place for everything. I wouldn’t write it off completely.